  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nOur first winter storm system is slowly moving off to the east \nthis morning. Lingering light rain should dwindle by late this \nmorning giving way to a dry Friday afternoon that will persist \ninto Saturday afternoon. Brief weak upper ridging will take hold\nbehind this system bringing warmer temperatures and sunnier \nskies across the region. Saturday and Sunday we may see \ntemperatures in the low to mid 50s. \n\nAnother round of showers is expected late Saturday night into \nSunday as the next trough is forecast to move through the Quad\nState area. Overall instability remains unimpressive, but \nmodels suggest mid-level lapse rates between 6-7.5C/km, which \nmay be just enough for a few thunderstorms among these showers. \nCurrently, the NBM suggests a quarter to half an inch of rain is\npossible from this system. Behind these showers, the associated\ncold front will move through Sunday afternoon bringing with it \nsome stronger 15-25 mph sustained winds across the area due to \nthe strong pressure gradient.\n\nSurface high pressure will build into the area next week with\nfairly zonal conditions aloft. Models are currently hinting at\ntemperatures warming into the low to mid 60s by mid to late next\nweek.",
